# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/004_qualifiers.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_qualifiers
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = const, volatile and restrict on objects and parameters
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No const object is written, and no const qualification is cast away and then written
through.  The restrict-qualified parameters are used truthfully: the destination and source
are distinct file-scope arrays that never alias, so the promise restrict makes is kept.
Every volatile access is to a real volatile object, and every index is inside bounds.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
const_read=42
volatile_seq=6
restrict_scaled=3 6 9 12 sum=30
const_qualified_ptr_target=42
const_pointer_write=19
END
